---
import '@fontsource/instrument-serif/400.css';
import '@fontsource/instrument-serif/400-italic.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/500.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/600.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css';
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import WaterBuilder from '../../components/coffee/WaterBuilder.svelte';
import { WATER_RECIPES, SOURCES } from '../../lib/coffee/index.ts';

const provenance = WATER_RECIPES.filter((r) => r.provenance).map((r) => r.provenance).join(' ');

const title = 'Coffee Water Recipe Builder: Make Your Own Brewing Water';
const description = 'See where your water sits among the well-known coffee water recipes, pick one, and get the exact mix: Epsom salt and baking soda concentrates in milliliters, or grams for a direct batch.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
		<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="JayArr Coffee: Deep Brew research" href="/deep-brew/rss.xml" />
		<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
			'@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: 'Water Recipe Builder', url: 'https://jayarr.coffee/deep-brew/water/', description,
			applicationCategory: 'UtilityApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' }, publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'JayArr Coffee', url: 'https://jayarr.coffee' },
		})}></script>
	</head>
	<body class="cf-page db-dark">
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="cf-intro">
				<a href="/deep-brew/" class="cf-back">← Deep Brew</a>
				<p class="cf-kicker">Deep Brew · Water Recipe Builder</p>
				<h1>Brewing water, <em>mixed to a recipe.</em></h1>
				<p class="cf-lede">Filter coffee is about 98% water. Pick a recipe, tell us what you start from, and get the exact mix, down to the milliliter.</p>
			</section>
			<section class="cf-tool" aria-label="Water Recipe Builder"><WaterBuilder client:load /><noscript><p class="tool-noscript">The interactive builder needs JavaScript. Below, the well-known coffee water recipes are listed in a table, with the two-bottle method for mixing them.</p></noscript></section>

			<article class="cf-body">
				<h2>Hardness and alkalinity, in plain words</h2>
				<p><strong>Hardness</strong> (GH) is mostly calcium and magnesium. They help pull flavor out of the coffee. Magnesium is "believed to extract slightly different flavors," Jonathan Gagné writes, though he knows of no lab tests that back this up yet. Too little and coffee tastes flat and thin; far too much and it turns chalky and scales machines.</p>
				<p><strong>Alkalinity</strong> (KH) is mostly bicarbonate. It buffers the coffee's acids: too little and the cup can taste sharp; too much and it tastes flat and dull. The map's dashed lines mark hardness two and three times the alkalinity; they're guides for reading the map, not a rule, and the recipes sit on both sides of them.</p>
				<p>Distilled and reverse-osmosis water have almost no minerals, which is why coffee brewed with them tastes hollow. Very hard tap water has the opposite problem.</p>

				<h2>Well-known coffee water recipes</h2>
				<div class="cf-table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead><tr><th scope="col">Recipe</th><th scope="col">GH</th><th scope="col">KH</th><th scope="col">Minerals</th><th scope="col">Good for</th></tr></thead>
						<tbody>{WATER_RECIPES.map((r) => <tr><th scope="row">{r.name}{r.untraced ? <small> (original not traced)</small> : ''}</th><td>{r.gh}{r.ghEstimated ? <small> (Builder's estimate)</small> : ''}</td><td>{r.kh}</td><td>{r.profile}</td><td>{r.bestFor}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<p class="cf-note">Hardness and alkalinity in ppm (mg/L) as calcium carbonate. These are each recipe's GH and KH targets; branded products such as Third Wave Water also contain other minerals that two salts don't copy. {provenance}</p>

				<h2>The two-bottle method</h2>
				<p>Barista Hustle's method keeps it simple. Dissolve 2.45 g of Epsom salt in a liter of distilled water (bottle A) and 1.68 g of baking soda in another (bottle B). Each milliliter of A in a liter of finished water adds about 1 ppm of hardness; each milliliter of B adds about 1 ppm of alkalinity. So the SCA's 68 GH / 40 KH is roughly 68 mL of A and 40 mL of B in a jug, filled with distilled water to the one-liter line. The builder above works out the exact amounts, including how much of your own water to use if you start from tap.</p>

				<h2>For espresso machines</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>Check your machine maker's water specification first. Harder water scales boilers faster, steam boilers most of all.</li>
					<li>Chloride corrodes stainless steel and copper under heat. Epsom salt (a sulfate) and baking soda add none; avoid calcium or magnesium chloride in machines.</li>
					<li>Softened water is a poor base: a softener swaps calcium and magnesium for sodium, removing the hardness that helps extraction.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Sources</h2>
				<ul class="cf-sources">
					{['barista-hustle-water', 'sca-water', 'hendon-2014', 'gagne-water', 'rao', 'synthesis'].map((id) => SOURCES[id]).map((s) => <li>{s.author}, <cite>{s.title}</cite>{s.year ? ` (${s.year})` : ''}.</li>)}
				</ul>
			</article>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style is:global>.cf-page { background: var(--t-paper, #f3ede3); }</style>
<style>
	main { color: var(--t-ink, #2a1d15); }
	.cf-intro { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 120px 24px 22px; }
	.cf-back { font: 500 12px/1 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--t-ink-3, #75655a); text-decoration: none; }
	.cf-kicker { font: 500 12px/1 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--t-crema-deep, #8f5d22); margin: 26px 0 0; }
	h1 { font: 400 clamp(40px, 6vw, 68px)/.98 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 10px 0 12px; }
	h1 em { font-style: italic; color: var(--t-coffee, #7a4e30); }
	.cf-lede { font: 400 17px/1.5 'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif; color: var(--t-ink-2, #5b4a3d); max-width: 60ch; margin: 0; }
	.cf-tool { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 8px 24px 40px; }
	.cf-body { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 24px 72px; font: 400 17px/1.7 'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif; }
	.cf-body h2 { font: 400 34px/1.1 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; margin: 44px 0 12px; }
	.cf-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--t-rule, #d9cdbd); border-radius: 12px; background: var(--t-card, #fbf8f3); }
	table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 15px; }
	th, td { text-align: left; padding: 10px 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--t-rule-2, #e6dccd); }
	thead th { font: 500 11px/1 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--t-ink-3, #75655a); }
	td:nth-child(2), td:nth-child(3) { font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; }
	tr:last-child th, tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
	.cf-note, .cf-sources { font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--t-ink-2, #5b4a3d); }
	@media (max-width: 640px) { .cf-intro { padding: 96px 16px 18px; } .cf-tool { padding: 4px 12px 32px; } .cf-body { padding: 16px 16px 56px; } }
</style>
